{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9654be952c05bb39", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tBreaking the Curse of Multilinguality in Many-to-Many Speech-to-Text\n2\tTranslation via a Resource-Aware Mixture of Speech Encoders\n3\tYexing Du1,2 , Kaiyuan Liu1,2 , Youcheng Pan2 , Bo Yang2 , Chengpeng Fu1,2 , Yu Wang2 , Ming Liu1,2\n4\t1\n5\t\n6\tHarbin Institute of Technology, Harbin, China\n7\t2\n8\tPengcheng Laboratory, Shenzhen, China\n9\t{yxdu, mliu}@ir.hit.edu.cn\n10\t\n11\tarXiv:2608.04586v1 [cs.CL] 5 Aug 2026\n12\t\n13\tAbstract\n14\tMultimodal large language models (MLLMs) have achieved\n15\tsignificant success in speech-to-text translation (S2TT). However, when processing multilingual speech inputs, a single\n16\tspeech encoder shared across all languages suffers from the\n17\tcurse of multilinguality: languages at different resource levels compete for limited representation capacity, leading to\n18\tstrong high-resource performance but substantial degradation on low-resource speech. To address this problem and\n19\timprove multilingual consistency, we propose MSRT, a novel\n20\tframework built around a resource-aware Mixture of Speech\n21\tEncoders (MoSE). MoSE uses an explicit language router\n22\tto assign each utterance to an appropriate expert encoder.\n23\tA frozen expert preserves high-resource language capabilities, while a trainable expert adapts to and specializes in\n24\tmedium- and low-resource languages. We further introduce a\n25\tfive-stage curriculum learning strategy that substantially reduces data dependence, requiring only 10 hours of paired\n26\tS2TT data per language for effective alignment. We conduct\n27\textensive experiments on 45 languages, systematically evaluating all 45 × 44 translation directions. Our 4B-parameter\n28\tmodel achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming\n29\tsubstantially larger baselines. Empirical analyses show that\n30\tMoSE improves high-, medium-, and low-resource languages\n31\tsimultaneously, with the largest gains on low-resource speech,\n32\tthereby breaking the curse of multilinguality without compromising high-resource performance. To support future multilingual S2TT research, we release our code and models.\n33\t\n34\tCode — https://github.com/yxduir/MSRT\n35\t\n36\tIntroduction\n37\tSpeech-to-text translation (S2TT) aims to translate speech\n38\tfrom a source language into text in a target language. Traditionally, these systems have utilized cascaded pipelines:\n39\tan Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) component first\n40\textracts a transcript from the speech (Radford et al. 2023),\n41\twhich a subsequent Machine Translation (MT) model then\n42\tconverts into the desired language (Shang et al. 2026). Recently, there has been a growing focus on integrating speech\n43\tencoders with large language models (LLMs) to endow\n44\tthem with multilingual speech capabilities, thereby advancing ASR (Ma et al. 2026) and S2TT tasks (Du et al. 2026).\n45\tThis manuscript is an arXiv preprint.\n46\t\n47\tFigure 1: Multilingual consistency by source language and\n48\tresource level. Consistency is the ratio of a source language’s\n49\taverage translation score (across 44 target directions) to the\n50\tEnglish average. Higher is better, and dashed vertical lines\n51\tindicate each model’s average within a resource group.\n52\t\n53\tDespite these advances, current MLLMs remain strongly\n54\tEnglish-centric in S2TT. They perform well on English-toX directions but degrade in many-to-many translation from\n55\tnon-English speech. As shown in Figure 1, their consistency\n56\tscores for low-resource speech inputs fall substantially below\n57\tthose for English, revealing a pronounced cross-lingual performance gap. This disparity is not merely a target-generation\n58\tissue: changing the source speech language causes substantial quality variation even when the target set and decoder remain unchanged. Consequently, English-centric results can\n59\toverstate multilingual coverage and obscure failures on multilingual speech inputs.\n60\t\n61\t\fOne important cause of this inconsistency is that most\n62\tmultilingual MLLMs (Xu et al. 2025) rely on a single shared\n63\tspeech encoder for all input languages. As language coverage expands, this architecture suffers from the “curse of\n64\tmultilinguality” (Conneau et al. 2020): limited encoder capacity forces languages with different levels of available resources to compete for the same representation space. Highresource languages require preserving pretrained acoustic\n65\tknowledge, whereas medium- and low-resource languages\n66\trequire greater adaptation. Joint optimization can therefore overwrite established representations or underfit scarcelanguage features.\n67\tInspired by the Mixture-of-Experts architectures in\n68\tLLMs (Blevins et al. 2024), we propose MSRT, an\n69\tS2TT MLLM centered on a resource-aware Mixture of\n70\tSpeech Encoders (MoSE). Two homogeneous Whisper encoders (Radford et al. 2023) assume asymmetric roles: a\n71\tfrozen expert handles high-resource languages, while a trainable expert adapts to medium- and low-resource speech. A\n72\tsource-language token routes each utterance to one expert,\n73\tand a target-language token controls generation. This design\n74\tprovides specialization at the inference cost of a single encoder. We further extend the curriculum strategy (Du et al.\n75\t2025) into five stages of increasing difficulty, progressively\n76\testablishing speech–text alignment and translation.\n77\tWe evaluate MSRT on FLEURS across all 45 × 44 nonidentity translation directions, with detailed comparisons on\n78\trepresentative languages from 11 language families. MSRT4B achieves the highest average COMET score of 83.3, outperforming both the Gemini-3.5-Flash-Lite API and the substantially larger MCAT-27B (Du et al. 2026). Across the\n79\tcomplete direction grid, MSRT obtains COMET scores of at\n80\tleast 80 on 1,552 directions (78.4%), compared with 928 for\n81\tGemini. Ablation studies show that MoSE improves performance across high-, medium-, and low-resource languages,\n82\twith the largest gains on low-resource languages, while further strengthening high-resource performance. These consistent gains across all resource groups demonstrate that MoSE\n83\tbreaks the curse of multilinguality rather than trading highresource performance for low-resource improvements. Experiments on CoVoST-2 further demonstrate robust crossdataset generalization.\n84\tOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n85\t• We propose MoSE, a resource-aware mixture-of-speechencoder architecture that breaks the curse of multilinguality by preserving high-resource knowledge while enabling targeted adaptation for medium- and low-resource\n86\tlanguages.\n87\t• We introduce MSRT-4B, a compact 4B-parameter model\n88\tthat supports all translation directions among 45 languages using only 10 hours of paired S2TT data per language. We open-source its code and model to facilitate\n89\treproducible research.\n90\t• We comprehensively evaluate all 1,980 translation directions across 45 languages, demonstrating broad and reliable many-to-many translation across diverse language\n91\tfamilies and resource levels, especially for low-resource\n92\tspeech in realistic multilingual settings.\n93\t\n94\tRelated Work\n95\tSpeech-to-Text Translation. Conventional speech-to-text\n96\ttranslation (S2TT) cascades automatic speech recognition\n97\tand machine translation. Although this design can reuse\n98\tstrong task-specific models, recognition errors propagate\n99\tdownstream and two-stage decoding increases latency. Endto-end S2TT instead learns a direct mapping from source\n100\tspeech to target text. SeamlessM4T integrates multilingual\n101\tspeech recognition and translation with text translation in a\n102\tsingle model, demonstrating that one architecture can support diverse speech–text tasks and languages (Barrault et al.\n103\t2023). Its success, however, still depends heavily on paired\n104\tsupervision. ZeroSwot reduces this dependence by connecting an ASR-trained speech encoder to a massively multilingual translation model through CTC compression and\n105\toptimal-transport alignment, enabling direct S2TT without\n106\tpaired translation data (Tsiamas et al. 2024). These studies establish speech–text alignment as a central challenge in\n107\tend-to-end translation.\n108\tMany-to-Many Speech-to-Text Translation. Many-tomany S2TT expands both source- and target-language coverage, requiring one model to preserve language-specific\n109\tacoustic cues while controlling multilingual generation.\n110\tSeamlessM4T demonstrates broad multilingual coverage\n111\tthrough shared recognition and translation components (Barrault et al. 2023). General-purpose multimodal LLMs such\n112\tas Qwen3-Omni further show that pretrained language backbones can support multilingual speech understanding and\n113\ttranslation (Xu et al. 2025). Specialized MLLMs more directly exploit the translation knowledge of LLMs: LLMSRT transfers it to many-to-many S2TT through curriculum\n114\tlearning (Du et al. 2025), while MCAT scales MLLM-based\n115\ttranslation to 70 languages (Du et al. 2026). Nevertheless,\n116\tincreasing language coverage does not eliminate resource imbalance or interference between languages. This limitation\n117\tmotivates speech-side mechanisms that allocate adaptation\n118\tcapacity according to the data resources and acoustic variation of different languages.\n119\tMixture of Experts. MoE has been explored in multimodal and speech models from several perspectives. UniMoE activates modality-specific experts to reduce crossmodal interference in a unified MLLM (Li et al. 2025),\n120\twhereas SimulMega uses expert routers as policies for balancing latency and quality in simultaneous translation (Le\n121\tet al. 2025). PaM selects heterogeneous audio encoders from\n122\ttask prompts to serve different audio tasks (Shan et al. 2025),\n123\twhile DialectMoE learns dynamic expert routing for multidialect ASR (Zhou et al. 2024). In contrast, this work explores MoE specifically for multilingual S2TT and focuses\n124\ton interference induced by imbalanced language resources.\n125\tMSRT routes source languages by resource level between\n126\ttwo homogeneous Whisper encoders with asymmetric roles:\n127\ta frozen expert preserves high-resource representations, and\n128\ta trainable expert specializes in medium- and low-resource\n129\tspeech. Thus, its explicit language-level routing investigates\n130\tmultilingual expert specialization without token-level load\n131\tbalancing or task-dependent expert selection.\n132\t\n133\t\fFigure 2: Overview of the MSRT framework. Based on the source-language token in the prompt, MoSE routes high-resource\n134\tspeech to a frozen encoder and medium- and low-resource speech to a trainable encoder.\n135\t\n136\tMethodology\n137\tModel Architecture\n138\tAs shown in Figure 2, MSRT connects a resource-aware\n139\tMixture of Speech Encoders (MoSE), a speech adapter, and\n140\ta pretrained LLM.\n141\tMixture of Speech Encoders. MoSE comprises two encoders and an explicit language router. Instead of sharing one\n142\tencoder across languages with unequal supervision, it separates high-resource knowledge preservation from mediumand low-resource adaptation while activating one expert per\n143\tutterance. This design breaks the curse of multilinguality by\n144\timproving underrepresented languages without sacrificing\n145\thigh-resource performance.\n146\t• Frozen Expert. The frozen expert Ef retains the original\n147\tWhisper encoder with fixed parameters. It preserves general speech representations for high-resource languages,\n148\tprevents catastrophic forgetting, and anchors the shared\n149\tspeech interface during adaptation.\n150\t• Trainable Expert. The trainable expert Et shares the\n151\tWhisper initialization but adapts to medium- and lowresource speech, capturing underrepresented accents and\n152\tphonological patterns. Its architecture matches Ef , so\n153\tboth experts produce compatible representations and\n154\tshare one adapter without expert-specific projections.\n155\t• Explicit Language Router. The router maps each\n156\tsource-language token to its resource-group expert: highresource languages use Ef , whereas medium- and lowresource languages use Et . Table 3 lists all assignments.\n157\tUtterance-level routing keeps all frames on one acoustic\n158\tpath and requires neither a learned gate nor a balancing\n159\tloss.\n160\t\n161\tOverall, let R(ℓs ) denote the expert selected for source\n162\tlanguage ℓs . The MoSE output is written as\n163\tHMoSE (x, ℓs ) = ER(ℓs ) (x),\n164\t\n165\tR(ℓs ) ∈ {f, t}.\n166\t\n167\t(1)\n168\t\n169\tHere, R(ℓs ) = f for ℓs ∈ Lhigh and R(ℓs ) = t otherwise,\n170\tcovering the medium- and low-resource groups. This formulation retains the inference cost of a single encoder.\n171\tSpeech Adapter. The selected encoder produces variablelength features. A Q-Former compresses them into a\n172\ttranslation-aware representation, while an MLP projects it\n173\tinto the LLM hidden space. Together, these modules remove\n174\tacoustic redundancy while retaining information required for\n175\ttranslation.\n176\tLLM. The pretrained LLM provides multilingual translation knowledge and remains frozen during speech-side alignment to prevent catastrophic forgetting. It is unlocked only in\n177\tthe final training stage, where it adapts to the aligned acoustic\n178\trepresentation.\n179\tTokenizer Expansion. We add dedicated language tokens\n180\tfor the FLEURS languages (Conneau et al. 2023). The\n181\tsource-language token controls MoSE encoder selection,\n182\twhile the target-language token specifies the LLM translation\n183\tdirection. Together, they provide explicit language control\n184\tand reduce unintended switching.\n185\tText Prompt. As shown in Figure 2 and Table 1, the prompt\n186\texplicitly instructs the LLM to translate from the source\n187\tlanguage into the target language. The source-language token\n188\talso controls encoder selection through the explicit language\n189\trouter, while the target-language token specifies the desired\n190\toutput language.\n191\t\n192\t\fTable 1: Prompt design for the five-stage curriculum.\n193\tStage Task Speech Text Prompt\n194\tI\n195\tII\n196\tIII\n197\tIV\n198\tV\n199\t\n200\tASR\n201\tASR\n202\tSMT\n203\tSRT\n204\tSRT\n205\t\n206\t✓\n207\t✓\n208\t✓\n209\t✓\n210\t✓\n211\t\n212\tPrediction\n213\t\n214\t<|eng|>\n215\tText\n216\t<|eng|>\n217\tText\n218\tText<|eng|><|cmn|> MT\n219\t<|eng|><|cmn|>\n220\tText<|eng|><|cmn|> MT\n221\t<|eng|><|cmn|>\n222\tText<|eng|><|cmn|> MT\n223\t\n224\tFive-Stage Curriculum Learning\n225\tFollowing Du et al. (2025), we organize ASR, speech-guided\n226\tmachine translation (SMT), and joint speech recognition and\n227\ttranslation (SRT) into the five stages in Table 1. Table 2 summarizes which modules are optimized at each stage; every\n228\tstage starts from the preceding checkpoint.\n229\t• Stage I: ASR alignment. The Q-Former and MLP\n230\tadapter are trained successively on English-only, highresource multilingual, and fully multilingual ASR data,\n231\testablishing a stable multilingual speech–text interface\n232\twhile both speech experts and the LLM remain frozen.\n233\t• Stage II: Expert specialization. MoSE routing is activated for multilingual ASR. The frozen expert Ef preserves high-resource representations, whereas Et is optimized for medium- and low-resource speech; the shared\n234\tadapter continues training.\n235\t• Stage III: Translation activation. SMT supplies the\n236\tground-truth transcription together with source- and\n237\ttarget-language tokens. The adapter is optimized to connect routed speech features to the translation knowledge\n238\tof the frozen LLM.\n239\t• Stage IV: End-to-end SRT. The ground-truth transcription is removed from the input. With the experts and LLM\n240\tfixed, the adapter learns to generate both the transcription\n241\tand translation directly from routed speech.\n242\t• Stage V: Joint adaptation. LLM LoRA is enabled and\n243\tjointly optimized with the adapter for SRT, adapting multilingual generation to acoustic inputs without updating\n244\tthe pretrained LLM weights.\n245\t\n246\tExperimental Settings\n247\tDatasets. We use Common Voice 24 (Ardila et al. 2020)\n248\tand FLEURS for multilingual ASR pre-training, combining\n249\tbroad language coverage with curated speech data. For S2TT\n250\ttraining, we use paired speech–text data from FLEURS, comprising approximately 10 hours of speech per language. We\n251\tevaluate the S2TT models on both FLEURS and the CoVoST2 (Wang et al. 2021) benchmark.\n252\tTraining Details. As shown in Table 2, the MLLM comprises a MiLMMT-4B LLM (Shang et al. 2026), MoSE, and\n253\ta speech adapter containing a Q-Former and an MLP. The\n254\tQ-Former uses 80 learnable queries to compress variablelength speech into a fixed-length interface for the LLM. We\n255\ttrain in BF16 with DeepSpeed ZeRO-0 and AdamW, using\n256\ta peak learning rate of 1 × 10−4 and 1,000 warmup steps.\n257\tTraining takes less than three days on eight Ascend 910C\n258\tNPUs. The same implementation can run on eight NVIDIA\n259\tA100 GPUs with comparable accuracy.\n260\tBaselines. We compare three representative deployment\n261\tparadigms:\n262\t• API-based: Gemini-3.5-Flash-Lite (Team et al. 2023)\n263\tserves as the API baseline.\n264\t• Cascade: Whisper-Large-v3 (Radford et al. 2023) first\n265\ttranscribes speech, after which NLLB-200-3.3B (NLLB\n266\tTeam et al. 2024) translates the transcription.\n267\t• End-to-end: SeamlessM4T-V2-Large (Barrault et al.\n268\t2023), Qwen3-Omni-30B (Xu et al. 2025), and MCAT27B (Du et al. 2026) directly generate translations from\n269\tspeech and cover both specialized and general-purpose\n270\tmultilingual architectures.\n271\tEvaluation Metrics. We employ COMET (Rei et al. 2022)\n272\tand spBLEU (Post 2018) as our evaluation metrics. Specifically, spBLEU uses the FLORES-200 tokenizer. All results\n273\tuse a beam size of 1 unless stated otherwise.\n274\tTable 3: Language support. Resource levels are defined following SeamlessM4T (Barrault et al. 2023).\n275\tResource Languages (ISO Code)\n276\tHigh\n277\t(Ef )\n278\t\n279\tArabic (ara), Bengali (ben), Catalan (cat)\n280\tCzech (ces), Chinese (cmn), German (deu)\n281\tEnglish (eng), Finnish (fin), French (fra)\n282\tItalian (ita), Japanese (jpn), Dutch (nld)\n283\tPolish (pol), Romanian (ron), Spanish (spa)\n284\t\n285\tMedium\n286\t(Et )\n287\t\n288\tDanish (dan), Greek (ell), Hindi (hin)\n289\tCroatian (hrv), Hungarian (hun), Indonesian (ind)\n290\tKazakh (kaz), Korean (kor), Portuguese (por)\n291\tRussian (rus), Slovak (slk), Tamil (tam)\n292\tTagalog (tgl), Thai (tha), Turkish (tur)\n293\tUrdu (urd), Uzbek (uzb), Vietnamese (vie)\n294\tAzerbaijani (azj), Bulgarian (bul), Persian (fas)\n295\tHebrew (heb), Khmer (khm), Lao (lao)\n296\tMalay (msa), Burmese (mya), Norwegian (nob)\n297\tSlovenian (slv), Swedish (swe), Cantonese (yue)\n298\t\n299\tTable 2: MLLM settings for MSRT.\n300\tModules\n301\t\n302\tStage\n303\t\n304\tDetails\n305\t\n306\tSpeech Encoder (MoSE)\n307\tFrozen Expert (Ef )\n308\tTrainable Expert (Et )\n309\tLanguage Router\n310\t\n311\t–\n312\tII\n313\t–\n314\t\n315\tFor high-resource\n316\tFor medium-/low-resource\n317\tExplicit expert routing\n318\t\n319\tSpeech Adapter\n320\tQ-Former\n321\tMLP\n322\t\n323\tAll\n324\tAll\n325\t\n326\tFeature compression\n327\tDimension alignment\n328\t\n329\tLLM\n330\t+ Vocabulary\n331\t+ LoRA\n332\t\n333\t–\n334\tV\n335\t\n336\tLanguage tokens\n337\tr = 16, α = 32\n338\t\n339\tLow\n340\t(Et )\n341\t\n342\tTotal\n343\t45 languages (15 high, 18 medium, and 12 low)\n344\tEf denotes the frozen expert, and Et denotes the trainable expert.\n345\t\n346\t\fTable 4: spBLEU / COMET results for 11 × 44 and 44 × 11 directions on FLEURS.\n347\tDirection\n348\t\n349\tGemini-3.5\n350\t-Flash-Lite\n351\t\n352\tWhisper+\n353\tNLLB-3.3B\n354\t\n355\tSeamlessM4T\n356\t-V2-Large\n357\t\n358\tQwen3-Omni\n359\t-30B-Instruct\n360\t\n361\tMCAT\n362\t-27B\n363\t\n364\tMSRT-4B\n365\t(ours)\n366\t\n367\tara→44\n368\tcmn→44\n369\teng→44\n370\thun→44\n371\tind→44\n372\tjpn→44\n373\tkor→44\n374\ttam→44\n375\ttha→44\n376\ttur→44\n377\tvie→44\n378\t\n379\t22.2 / 80.2\n380\t17.2 / 79.5\n381\t34.2 / 86.6\n382\t14.8 / 70.6\n383\t27.4 / 84.6\n384\t18.4 / 80.8\n385\t20.8 / 83.1\n386\t17.3 / 78.9\n387\t20.2 / 82.0\n388\t25.6 / 83.7\n389\t19.7 / 80.1\n390\t\n391\t21.1 / 78.7\n392\t18.5 / 80.5\n393\t30.5 / 84.3\n394\t21.2 / 79.9\n395\t23.7 / 82.2\n396\t18.9 / 80.5\n397\t19.5 / 81.8\n398\t12.9 / 71.4\n399\t17.1 / 78.6\n400\t23.4 / 83.0\n401\t18.7 / 78.9\n402\t\n403\t15.7 / 70.1\n404\t13.4 / 74.0\n405\t31.8 / 85.3\n406\t15.3 / 68.9\n407\t15.2 / 71.5\n408\t11.9 / 69.5\n409\t14.2 / 74.1\n410\t12.6 / 69.8\n411\t11.5 / 69.0\n412\t15.8 / 72.1\n413\t14.1 / 72.6\n414\t\n415\t20.5 / 78.8\n416\t20.9 / 82.8\n417\t31.9 / 85.7\n418\t11.0 / 65.0\n419\t24.7 / 83.4\n420\t19.5 / 81.8\n421\t20.4 / 82.7\n422\t2.5 / 50.4\n423\t18.8 / 81.3\n424\t23.2 / 82.5\n425\t20.0 / 81.3\n426\t\n427\t20.0 / 79.7\n428\t17.7 / 81.3\n429\t31.9 / 87.1\n430\t19.6 / 79.9\n431\t24.2 / 84.2\n432\t18.0 / 80.8\n433\t20.9 / 83.4\n434\t13.0 / 74.0\n435\t15.6 / 78.9\n436\t24.0 / 84.1\n437\t18.0 / 80.5\n438\t\n439\t23.7 / 82.0\n440\t20.8 / 83.2\n441\t34.2 / 87.9\n442\t23.8 / 83.4\n443\t27.3 / 85.4\n444\t20.4 / 82.3\n445\t22.4 / 84.2\n446\t17.9 / 79.4\n447\t20.8 / 82.9\n448\t25.7 / 85.2\n449\t21.4 / 82.7\n450\t\n451\t44→cmn\n452\t44→eng\n453\t44→hun\n454\t44→ind\n455\t44→jpn\n456\t44→kor\n457\t44→tam\n458\t44→tha\n459\t44→tur\n460\t44→vie\n461\t\n462\t21.1 / 78.3\n463\t24.9 / 79.2\n464\t20.6 / 79.7\n465\t21.7 / 80.9\n466\t18.2 / 81.8\n467\t16.0 / 79.9\n468\t20.0 / 80.0\n469\t27.6 / 78.1\n470\t19.6 / 78.4\n471\t23.5 / 79.4\n472\t\n473\t14.3 / 71.2\n474\t31.7 / 80.5\n475\t18.4 / 75.7\n476\t22.9 / 80.5\n477\t9.3 / 76.1\n478\t13.9 / 77.5\n479\t19.8 / 78.5\n480\t20.2 / 74.0\n481\t18.3 / 76.0\n482\t24.0 / 78.7\n483\t\n484\t11.3 / 65.4\n485\t31.1 / 83.3\n486\t13.8 / 67.9\n487\t16.1 / 76.7\n488\t7.3 / 71.9\n489\t10.2 / 71.8\n490\t17.6 / 75.9\n491\t20.1 / 70.3\n492\t13.6 / 70.0\n493\t18.4 / 73.8\n494\t\n495\t21.3 / 78.4\n496\t28.1 / 79.7\n497\t15.9 / 74.4\n498\t21.0 / 79.0\n499\t17.2 / 81.0\n500\t15.2 / 78.7\n501\t15.1 / 74.7\n502\t26.7 / 78.1\n503\t15.8 / 73.8\n504\t22.2 / 77.3\n505\t\n506\t21.5 / 80.2\n507\t27.5 / 80.6\n508\t17.8 / 79.7\n509\t21.9 / 82.1\n510\t18.2 / 83.1\n511\t15.5 / 80.7\n512\t19.0 / 81.0\n513\t26.2 / 79.8\n514\t18.6 / 78.9\n515\t23.2 / 81.1\n516\t\n517\t25.2 / 83.3\n518\t34.0 / 84.1\n519\t21.4 / 82.4\n520\t25.3 / 85.0\n521\t19.9 / 85.4\n522\t16.8 / 83.2\n523\t21.2 / 83.1\n524\t29.9 / 82.5\n525\t21.1 / 81.6\n526\t27.1 / 83.9\n527\t\n528\t20.3 / 81.3\n529\t21.0 / 80.5\n530\t20.6 / 80.9\n531\t\n532\t23.5 / 83.5\n533\t24.1 / 83.2\n534\t23.8 / 83.3\n535\t\n536\t11→44\n537\t21.6 / 80.9\n538\t20.5 / 80.0\n539\t15.6 / 72.4\n540\t19.4 / 77.8\n541\t44→11\n542\t21.1 / 79.1\n543\t19.4 / 76.8\n544\t16.0 / 72.6\n545\t19.8 / 77.2\n546\t20.0 / 78.4\n547\t15.8 / 72.5\n548\t19.6 / 77.5\n549\tAvg.\n550\t21.3 / 80.0\n551\tUnderlining denotes the second-best results, while bold blue values indicate the best performance.\n552\t\n553\tExperiments\n554\tMain Results\n555\tLanguage Selection. As shown in Table 4, the 11 languages cover 11 language families: Arabic (Afro-Asiatic),\n556\tMandarin Chinese (Sino-Tibetan), English (Indo-European),\n557\tHungarian (Uralic), Indonesian (Austronesian), Japanese\n558\t(Japonic), Korean (Koreanic), Tamil (Dravidian), Thai (Kra–\n559\tDai), Turkish (Turkic), and Vietnamese (Austroasiatic).\n560\tOverall Analysis. According to the overall average in\n561\tTable 4, MSRT-4B achieves the highest COMET score\n562\tof 83.3, outperforming MCAT-27B (80.9), Gemini-3.5Flash-Lite (80.0), Whisper+NLLB-200-3.3B (78.4), Qwen3Omni (77.5), and SeamlessM4T-V2-Large (72.5). Moreover,\n563\tMSRT-4B ranks first in both the 11 × 44 and 44 × 11 settings, showing that its overall advantage is sustained across\n564\ttranslation directions rather than driven by performance in a\n565\tsingle setting.\n566\tTranslation Direction Analysis. Performance differs notably between 11 × 44 and 44 × 11 directions. The cascaded system achieves a competitive COMET score of 80.0\n567\tin the former setting but drops to 76.8 in the latter, indicating\n568\tthat translation from a broader set of source speech languages is more challenging, partly due to accumulated multilingual recognition errors. In contrast, MSRT-4B achieves\n569\tCOMET scores of 83.5 and 83.2, respectively, demonstrating the strongest performance and robust consistency across\n570\ttranslation directions.\n571\t\n572\tEng→44 S2TT\n573\tOverall Analysis of Eng→44 Directions. Table 5 reports\n574\tCOMET performance across all 44 English-source translation directions, including the newly evaluated Gemini-3.5Flash-Lite baseline. MSRT-4B achieves the highest average\n575\tscore of 87.9, outperforming MCAT-27B, Gemini-3.5-FlashLite, Qwen3-Omni, and SeamlessM4T-V2-Large by 0.8, 1.3,\n576\t2.2, and 2.6 points, respectively. These results demonstrate\n577\tthe strong and consistent English-to-many translation performance of MSRT-4B.\n578\tEnglish-centric vs. Many-to-Many Translation. Although optimized for many-to-many translation, MSRT-4B\n579\talso performs strongly on English-source directions, achieving an average COMET score of 87.9 on eng→44 and an\n580\toverall score of 83.3 in Table 4. These results suggest that\n581\tfuture speech translation models should emphasize comprehensive multilingual optimization rather than focusing predominantly on English-centric directions.\n582\tHigh-Resource vs. Low-Resource Languages. Table 5\n583\tshows that Qwen3-Omni performs strongly on high-resource\n584\tlanguages such as Chinese (cmn) and Japanese (jpn), but degrades markedly on low-resource languages such as Burmese\n585\t(mya) and Khmer (khm). In comparison, MSRT-4B maintains consistently strong performance across both resource\n586\tregimes. This advantage stems from MoSE, whose trainable\n587\texpert specializes in medium- and low-resource languages,\n588\twhile its frozen expert preserves high-resource performance.\n589\t\n590\t\fTable 5: COMET results on English → 44 directions.\n591\tISO\n592\t\n593\tGemini-3.5 Seamless Qwen3 MCAT MSRT\n594\t-Flash-Lite\n595\tM4T\n596\t-Omni -27B\n597\t-4B\n598\t\n599\tara\n600\tazj\n601\tben\n602\tbul\n603\tcat\n604\tces\n605\tcmn\n606\tdan\n607\tdeu\n608\tell\n609\tfas\n610\tfin\n611\tfra\n612\theb\n613\thin\n614\thrv\n615\thun\n616\tind\n617\tita\n618\tjpn\n619\tkaz\n620\tkhm\n621\tkor\n622\tlao\n623\tmsa\n624\tmya\n625\tnld\n626\tnob\n627\tpol\n628\tpor\n629\tron\n630\trus\n631\tslk\n632\tslv\n633\tspa\n634\tswe\n635\ttam\n636\ttha\n637\ttgl\n638\ttur\n639\turd\n640\tuzb\n641\tvie\n642\tyue\n643\t\n644\t83.8\n645\t85.8\n646\t85.1\n647\t88.9\n648\t85.3\n649\t89.1\n650\t86.4\n651\t88.7\n652\t86.2\n653\t87.1\n654\t86.5\n655\t90.9\n656\t85.8\n657\t85.2\n658\t79.0\n659\t89.0\n660\t88.1\n661\t89.8\n662\t86.3\n663\t89.7\n664\t88.6\n665\t81.4\n666\t88.4\n667\t81.4\n668\t87.6\n669\t87.1\n670\t85.5\n671\t87.9\n672\t87.1\n673\t87.3\n674\t88.5\n675\t87.8\n676\t88.4\n677\t87.8\n678\t84.2\n679\t88.7\n680\t87.6\n681\t87.1\n682\t82.9\n683\t88.3\n684\t81.2\n685\t88.2\n686\t87.1\n687\t81.7\n688\t\n689\t84.5\n690\t83.8\n691\t84.6\n692\t88.8\n693\t85.0\n694\t88.0\n695\t79.7\n696\t88.7\n697\t84.9\n698\t87.5\n699\t84.6\n700\t88.5\n701\t85.3\n702\t84.5\n703\t78.1\n704\t87.8\n705\t86.0\n706\t89.0\n707\t85.1\n708\t84.7\n709\t87.9\n710\t79.9\n711\t85.1\n712\t81.4\n713\t86.6\n714\t85.7\n715\t85.1\n716\t86.8\n717\t85.7\n718\t86.6\n719\t87.8\n720\t86.3\n721\t87.3\n722\t86.8\n723\t83.2\n724\t88.4\n725\t87.3\n726\t82.1\n727\t83.3\n